 "licence_note": null,
 "history": "The Ottoman, Levantine, Armenian and Egyptian men's trousers were cut very wide, of wool broadcloth or a coarse woollen (red broadcloth for the better-off, camlet in summer), drawn on a cord at the waist, the seat hanging low and the legs gathered into a band at the ankle; the Persian and Kurdish kind were narrower, 'loose ... without any tight ligaments' [NIE][RUS][HAN][CHE].",
